# Copy to `.env.local`. Never commit `.env.local`.
#
# WARNING: every EXPO_PUBLIC_* value is inlined into the JavaScript bundle and can be
# read out of a shipped app. These are CONFIG, not secrets. A secret that a mobile
# client needs is a design error — it belongs behind the backend. See CLAUDE.md Safety.

# The first three values are REQUIRED and validated at launch by src/shared/config/env.ts. A
# missing or malformed one stops the app on a startup screen naming it, rather than
# failing later as a confusing error inside a feature. The fourth, the link domain, is
# optional and stays empty until a domain exists.

# EXPO_PUBLIC_ENV — one of: local | development | staging | production
#
# The same four names the backend and the club app use. **Only `local` runs today.**
# `development` and `staging` are defined and validated but not provisioned — no domain
# is registered, so no host exists for either — and there is no production deployment.
#
# The environment decides the URL scheme EXPO_PUBLIC_API_URL and EXPO_PUBLIC_FEATURES_URL
# must use, and that is a validated rule rather than a convention. Both URLs are reached
# FROM A PHONE, OVER THE INTERNET, so the deployed environments get the strict form of the
# rule:
#
#   local        http:// or https://   a laptop, a LAN IP, or a tunnel
#   development  https://              deployed, not provisioned yet
#   staging      https://              deployed, not provisioned yet
#   production   https://              deployed, does not exist yet
#
# `local` takes either: `expo start --tunnel`, cloudflared and tailscale funnel all hand
# back an https:// address, and a tunnel is exactly what a physical phone that cannot
# reach your LAN IP needs. Nothing about a laptop is protected by forbidding TLS on it.
#
# So setting EXPO_PUBLIC_ENV to anything but `local` today fails at launch, because the
# only reachable backend is the http:// one below. That is the truth, and it is better
# than a build that silently points somewhere wrong.
#
# It is also read by app.config.ts, in Node, before the app starts: it decides the name
# under the icon (`Rakieta local`, bare `Rakieta` in production) and whether the amber
# environment badge shows. A missing or misspelled value stops `expo start` naming this
# variable, rather than building an app called after the wrong environment.
EXPO_PUBLIC_ENV=local

# FastAPI base URL. On a physical device this must be a LAN IP or a tunnel, not
# localhost — the phone's localhost is the phone.
EXPO_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://localhost:8000

# The feature-flag datafiles' base URL (docs/adr/0206). The app fetches
# `<this>/<EXPO_PUBLIC_ENV>/featurevisor-player.json` at launch and on every return to the
# foreground, and never waits for it: with none fetched it uses the last one it cached, and
# with none cached, each feature's code default.
#
# Locally, run `npm run serve` in the flags repo (Rakieta02/rakieta-features), which serves
# the built datafiles over plain HTTP on port 3040 on all interfaces. The same rule as the
# API URL applies: on a physical device this is your machine's LAN IP, never localhost —
# EXPO_PUBLIC_FEATURES_URL=http://<your LAN IP>:3040. With nothing served at all, every
# feature is at its code default, which is how the app ships. Later it is the bucket the
# flags repo's publish run uploads to — a changed value, no code.
EXPO_PUBLIC_FEATURES_URL=http://localhost:3040

# The web domain the app's links live on — a bare host such as rakieta.app, with no scheme,
# port or path — or EMPTY, which is every environment today (#520, docs/adr/0187 §7).
#
# A group link (/join/<code>), a friend link (/f/<code>), the invitation email's button
# (/invite/<token>) and the reset email's link (/reset/<token>) are pages of the public site. On
# a phone with Rakieta installed they open the app instead: by the app's own scheme,
# rakieta://join/<code>, always — and by this domain once it is set, because app.config.ts then
# tells iOS (an associated domain) and Android (a verified intent filter) that the domain's four
# paths are the app's. Setting it needs a native rebuild, and the public site must serve the
# association files for it (frontend_public, #526); a domain claimed with no site behind it opens
# the browser.
#
# Read by app.config.ts in Node and by src/shared/config/env.ts at launch, through one
# function (src/shared/config/appLinks.ts), so the binary and the running app agree. A
# malformed value stops both, naming this variable.
EXPO_PUBLIC_LINK_DOMAIN=
